---
// The Cloud skill's showcase: a 7-step engineering-process pipeline —
// what has to be true through building it so it repeats — not a comparison
// of deployment targets. Production (EKS) and the physical backup machine
// still appear, as evidence inside steps 3 and 5, the same way QA's bug
// report is evidence inside a step rather than the card's organizing
// structure. K3s gets one clause, deliberately: it's a lab, not one of the
// two things a player's traffic actually touches, so it isn't a pillar here.
//
// Box text stays plain-English on purpose — this is the one place a
// non-technical reader has to follow the shape of the argument without
// AWS vocabulary. The technical specifics (OIDC, ElastiCache, security
// groups, Terraform) live one layer down, in the card's `details[].body`.
//
// Coordinates are deliberately reused, not invented: row 1 is QA's row-1
// geometry (178-wide boxes), row 2 is AI's row-2 geometry (246-wide boxes),
// snaking right at the same drop point AI uses. Same visual language, same
// text budget (178w ≈ 22/26 chars, 246w ≈ 30/36 chars).
// Source facts: src/content/cards/cloud.md, docs/context/deployment.md,
// docs/context/decisions.md.
---

<figure class="card-diagram">
  <div class="card-diagram-scroll">
    <svg
      class="topology"
      viewBox="0 0 820 400"
      role="img"
      aria-labelledby="cloud-pipeline-title cloud-pipeline-desc"
      xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
      fill="none"
    >
      <title id="cloud-pipeline-title">Seven steps from a real requirement to infrastructure that runs itself</title>
      <desc id="cloud-pipeline-desc">
        Seven steps, in order. One: decide what has to be true before any
        server exists — a real cost limit, and exactly when the expensive
        setup actually needs to exist. Two: decide who's allowed to touch it,
        using access that proves itself each time and can expire, not a
        saved password. Three: build it in two genuinely different places —
        a full production setup, and a physical machine at home that serves
        the real demo — kept completely separate. There's also a small
        always-on lab where changes get tried first, but it never serves a
        player directly. Four: make it shut itself off on a schedule,
        because a spending alert can arrive too late to matter. Five: only
        pay for the expensive pieces where the size of the job actually
        calls for them. Six: get a second, human look before trusting it —
        that's what caught an AWS default that quietly changes behaviour
        without ever raising an error. Seven: build it so it repeats itself
        exactly, with a check that stops a deploy if the infrastructure
        that's supposed to be running was never actually applied. From
        there, it's ready for the DevOps card's release pipeline.
      </desc>

      <defs>
        <marker id="cloud-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse">
          <path class="arrow-head" d="M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z" stroke="none" />
        </marker>
      </defs>

      <g class="hotspot" data-detail="objectives" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-label="Step 1, what must be true before any server exists — open the explanation">
        <rect class="node" x="15" y="40" width="178" height="84" rx="10" />
        <text class="label" x="29" y="64">1 · What must be true</text>
        <text class="sub" x="29" y="82">a cost limit, set first</text>
        <text class="sub" x="29" y="98">before any server exists</text>
      </g>

      <path class="link" d="M193,82 L219,82" marker-end="url(#cloud-arrow)" />

      <g class="hotspot" data-detail="foundation" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-label="Step 2, who's allowed to touch it — open the explanation">
        <rect class="node" x="219" y="40" width="178" height="84" rx="10" />
        <text class="label" x="233" y="64">2 · Who's allowed in</text>
        <text class="sub" x="233" y="82">no shared passwords</text>
        <text class="sub" x="233" y="98">access that can expire</text>
      </g>

      <path class="link" d="M397,82 L423,82" marker-end="url(#cloud-arrow)" />

      <g class="hotspot" data-detail="environments" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-label="Step 3, where it actually runs — open the explanation">
        <rect class="node" x="423" y="40" width="178" height="84" rx="10" />
        <text class="label" x="437" y="64">3 · Where it runs</text>
        <text class="sub" x="437" y="82">one built for production</text>
        <text class="sub" x="437" y="98">one already paid for</text>
      </g>

      <path class="link" d="M601,82 L627,82" marker-end="url(#cloud-arrow)" />

      <g class="hotspot" data-detail="guardrails" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-label="Step 4, it shuts itself off on schedule — open the explanation">
        <rect class="node" x="627" y="40" width="178" height="84" rx="10" />
        <text class="label" x="641" y="64">4 · Shuts itself off</text>
        <text class="sub" x="641" y="82">on a set schedule</text>
        <text class="sub" x="641" y="98">not waiting on an alert</text>
      </g>

      <path class="link" d="M716,124 L716,230" marker-end="url(#cloud-arrow)" />

      <g class="hotspot" data-detail="resources" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-label="Step 5, only paying for what the job needs — open the explanation">
        <rect class="node" x="559" y="230" width="246" height="84" rx="10" />
        <text class="label" x="573" y="254">5 · Pay only when needed</text>
        <text class="sub" x="573" y="272">a bigger machine, sometimes</text>
        <text class="sub" x="573" y="288">a smaller one, always on</text>
      </g>

      <path class="link" d="M559,272 L533,272" marker-end="url(#cloud-arrow)" />

      <g class="hotspot" data-detail="validation" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-label="Step 6, checked before it's trusted — open the explanation">
        <rect class="node" x="287" y="230" width="246" height="84" rx="10" />
        <text class="label" x="301" y="254">6 · Checked before trusted</text>
        <text class="sub" x="301" y="272">a hidden default, caught early</text>
        <text class="sub" x="301" y="288">before anyone relied on it</text>
      </g>

      <path class="link" d="M287,272 L261,272" marker-end="url(#cloud-arrow)" />

      <g class="hotspot" data-detail="automation" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-label="Step 7, built so it repeats itself — open the explanation">
        <rect class="node" x="15" y="230" width="246" height="84" rx="10" />
        <text class="label" x="29" y="254">7 · Built to repeat itself</text>
        <text class="sub" x="29" y="272">one recipe, every time</text>
        <text class="sub" x="29" y="288">no manual steps to forget</text>
      </g>

      <a class="card-jump" href="#card-devops" aria-label="Continue to the DevOps card, where this becomes a deploy pipeline">
        <path class="link" d="M138,314 L138,338" marker-end="url(#cloud-arrow)" />
        <text class="wire" x="150" y="332">ready for DevOps CI/CD</text>
        <text class="wire" x="150" y="346">&amp; application deployment</text>
      </a>

      <text class="wire accent" x="15" y="368">AI compared costs and drafted the trade-off tables</text>
      <text class="wire" x="15" y="384">which one shipped, and which one didn't, was never its call</text>
    </svg>
  </div>
  <figcaption class="card-diagram-caption">
    Click any step to read what it means.
  </figcaption>
</figure>
